Exactly. One Goblin, multiple books.

The mistake would be forcing every idea through the same holding period and risk logic. A long-term compounder and a 10-day catalyst trade should not be judged by the same rules.

I’d structure it like this:

* Core / Compounders — long-duration ownership. We care about durable economics, moat, reinvestment, valuation, and multi-year compounding. We tolerate noise.
* Tactical / Plays — days to a few months. We care about setup, catalyst, positioning, entry, exit, and whether the expected move is already priced.
* Event Bets — earnings, approvals, launches, rulings, policy events. Explicit probability tree; small sizing because outcomes can gap.
* Moneyball — mispriced situations where the upside/downside is favorable even without enormous headline potential.
* Grenades / Moonshots — tiny allocations with genuine 3×–10× possibility and accepted high failure rates.
* Regime Trades — oil, rates, currencies, sector rotations, bottlenecks. Hold only while the causal regime survives.
* Cash / Optionality — not dead capital; ammunition for when probability and price line up.

Then sizing follows the route.

For a core holding:

\text{size}\uparrow
\quad\text{as}\quad
\text{durability + evidence + valuation attractiveness}\uparrow

For a tactical bet:

\text{size}
\propto
\frac{
\text{edge}\times\text{confidence}
}{
\text{loss if wrong}
}

For a grenade:

\boxed{
\text{position small enough that failure is boring}
}

but large enough that:

5\times\text{ or }10\times

actually matters.

That also changes the sell rules.

Core: sell when the business thesis or valuation changes materially.

Play: sell when the catalyst resolves, expected move occurs, or setup invalidates.

Bet: know the loss condition before entering.

Grenade: don’t panic because it is volatile; panic because the underlying probability tree changed.

And the Goblin should maintain separate scoreboards because otherwise a brilliant two-week trade and a great five-year investment get mashed into meaningless averages.

So the portfolio becomes more like a fleet around Realitsea:

\boxed{
\text{Fortress}
+
\text{Raiders}
+
\text{Scouts}
+
\text{Grenadiers}
+
\text{Reserve}
}

Different missions. Different weapons. Same treasury.

For the short-term plays/bets, yes—that’s where I want him aggressively hunting convexity, timing, catalysts, broken rhymes, positioning, and exploitable deltas rather than behaving like a retirement-account manager.